WarpAxe SHA bench: the SHA peripheral measured over the Bitcoin genesis header
chip esp32s3 v0.2, 2 cores, CPU 240 MHz, APB 80 MHz, XTAL 40 MHz, ESP-IDF v5.3.3, xtensa
software reference against the genesis hash: ok
a run takes about a second and repeats every 30 s; the SHABENCH line is the machine-readable result

==== SHA bench run 1: esp32s3 v0.2, 2 cores, CPU 240 MHz, APB 80 MHz, XTAL 40 MHz, ESP-IDF v5.3.3 (xtensa) ====
T  register access, CPU cycles, best of 8 with interrupts off
   two cycle-counter reads back to back        1
   16 stores to TEXT                         103   6.4 a store
   the same with MEMW/FENCE after each       333   20.8 a store
   8 stores to H                              70   8.8 a store
   8 loads from H                            264   33.0 a load
   one BUSY load                              18
   a TEXT store, then a BUSY load             36
   16 stores to internal RAM (for scale)     102
A  how long a trigger keeps the engine busy, cycles
   CONTINUE -> idle after 18 stores          283 polled      244 as a counted delay
   START -> idle after 18 stores             283 polled      244 as a counted delay
   CONTINUE -> idle, bus drained first       165 polled      140 as a counted delay   (the engine alone)
B  when the engine samples TEXT
   TEXT[0] TEXT[3] TEXT[8] TEXT[15] safe to overwrite after   0 0 0 0 cycles   (0 = latched at the trigger)
   a block leaves TEXT as written         yes
D  the DMA path (a DMA channel on the SHA trigger): ok
   DMA_START one block -> idle               399
   DMA_START two blocks -> idle              525
   two-block DMA hash = midstate path     yes
   TEXT holds the DMA'd block afterwards  yes
V  loop variants, 2048 nonces each, every nonce re-hashed in software (bad must be 0)
   #  variant                                                cycles/nonce     kH/s   bad
   0  the loop as first shipped (store, trigger, poll, load)      888         270.3   0
   1  next block's words written while the engine runs          745         322.1   0
   2  1 with the digest read in one go (8 loads, 8 stores)      787         305.0   0
   3  1 with a counted wait before the first BUSY read          766         313.3   0
   4  2 and 3 together                                          805         298.1   0
   5  the WarpAxe firmware loop (wa_sha256d_hw_scan)            752         319.1   0
   counted wait of 3 and 4: 140 cycles; variants 1-5 pipelined (the engine latches TEXT at the trigger)
   the firmware loop found the genesis block: nonce 0x7c2bac1d at index 1000, hash as expected
